00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.389 align:start position:0%
 
I'm joined today by mark Rosenberg who's  the founding director of the CDC's  National Center for injury prevention  and control also a retired assistant  Surgeon General Mark it's great to have  you back you know there have been some  changes reported in the in media about  the extent to which the government and  CDC can research gun violence this is a  topic that's been very controversial for  some time so let's start sort of with  the basic facts what has changed and  what hasn't changed with regard to the  CDC's ability to research gun violence  well thanks David this is really an  important topic I think what has changed  is that people are starting to  understand how important it is to bring  science back into this issue and what  happened was marked in the last omnibus  appropriations bill that was passed by  Congress and then signed by the  President and this bill had language in  it that quoted the new secretary  secretary a czar of Health and Human  Services  he said the Dickey amendment does not  prohibit CDC from researching gun  violence prevention that was a very big  step because there was a lot of  misunderstanding before people thought  that the Dickey amendment was a  prohibition on the research and it  absolutely was not what it was was when  it was passed it was a compromised in  1996 the compromise between the NRA net  faction which wanted to shut down the  whole Injury Center and stop the  research and those people in Congress  who wanted the research to go on the  compromise was the wording of the Dickey  amendment that said none of these funds  that go to CDC can be used to promote or  advocate gun control it didn't say  anything about research and in fact CDC  was not in the business of advocating  gun control now that's becoming clear  and that position had  endorsement from the Trump  administration and from Congress it was  a very big and important step so  strictly speaking the CDC never was  actually banned from doing research on  gun violence but politically there was  an environment in which effectively is  it fair to say they were doing no such  research well CDC had been doing the  research but the NRA got very angry at  CDC because the first research study  that CDC published looked at the  question of whether having a gun in your  home makes you safer as the NRA  advertised they said if you care about  your family's safety you'll have a gun  in your home if you're a real man you'll  have a gun in your home so CDC looked at  the question of whether in fact having a  gun in your home made you safer and what  we found was that having a gun in your  home not only didn't it make you safer  but in increase the risk that someone in  your home would die from a gun homicide  not by a little bit not by 10 or 20  percent but by 200 percent that's a huge  increase in risk and then increased the  risk of that someone would die from gun  suicide by 5 times or 400% the NRA  didn't like that and got very angry and  wanted to get CDC out of the business  they told people everyone in Congress  and all of their members that you have a  choice either you do the research or you  keep your guns but you can't do both so  they mounted a huge campaign against CDC  and the Dickey amendment was basically a  shot across the bow that said if you do  research in this area we will make your  life miserable  yeah by directing a letter to you  stating you're violating the terms so  there's a concern about the language  that now is present about this which is  that it appears at least to limit the  the ways in which the funding can be  used  examining the causes of gun violence but  arguably restricting or preventing the  CDC from looking at ways to prevent or  solve the issue of gun violence is that  relevant because you did say just a few  minutes ago that that's not typically  what the CDC would do anyway that's not  really the business that they're in so  is that concern about the language  relevant or not I don't think that's a  legitimate restriction on the scope of  the research the restriction says CDC  can't lobby for gun control legislation  CDC never has and doesn't intend to  lobby what they intend to do is  basically look at the research to answer  four simple questions what's the problem  what are the causes what works to  prevent it and how do you scale it up  and translate it into legislation and  policy and I don't think there's any  restriction on that you've been  following this issue for a very very  long time do you believe that since the  parklands shooting and the huge influx  of student activism and sort of a  renewed interest in this topic which we  often see after a mass shooting but it  seems to be happening even more right  now do you think anything is actually  different in terms of the sort of  legislative and elected official will to  actually make changes and deal with this  at this point or do you see it as mostly  political posturing oh no I think  there's there's been a real shift and  two things have happened one is I think  people are starting to understand what  does research have to do with getting us  out of this horrible horrible deadly  morass people understand that research  can lead us out in five ways that will  work to both reduce gun violence and to  protect gun rights if you take guns out  of the hands of people who've already  been convicted of domestic violence  felonies are misdemeanors you're not  interfering with the rights of  law-abiding gun owners there are many  ways to differentiate who has guns and  to start to take them out of the hands  of people who legally should not have  them so there are ways to do both and I  think  it's become clear that it's not either  or it's not either prevent gun violence  through research or keep your guns but  there are ways to do both so that's a  big difference the second thing is that  I think even the people in the gun  rights camp don't want their children to  be killed at school they don't want  their children to be killed at shopping  malls or at waffle houses or at concerts  no one wants that no one wants to live  in fear of mass killings no one wants to  see veterans committing gun suicide  whether you're pro-gun rights or pro-gun  control yeah I mean know what I  generally agree with that here's my  concern though mark right like let's I  attribute good faith to the overwhelming  majority of the people that are on the  side of gun rights it's not that they  they they want to prevent kids from  being killed and isn't people from being  killed of course they do the part where  I think it gets complex is they don't  see some kids being killed or some  innocent people people being killed or  whatever as enough to override the  Second Amendment as they perceive it in  context of sort of America and the  Constitution so it's not to me an issue  of they're indifferent to the deaths of  kids it's that they see the actions that  some want to take myself included to  protect kids and innocent people as  pushing too far or pushing too hard  against the Second Amendment and I don't  know how we can actually have a  productive conversation if that's  someone's point of view I think there  are people who still believe what the  old NRA leadership told them that if you  do this research you will lose all your  guns what's becoming clear is that  that's not true this was a lie that was  fed to the membership and to legislators  by the NRA leadership who wanted to stop  the research at all costs and now I  think NRA members overwhelmingly want to  do this  search to find ways that can both  protect their rights and reduce gun  violence I think that's a big change and  it's not either/or in fact if we don't  do the research we probably won't be  able to protect gun rights as well as we  could if we do the research and find out  what works to do both there are so many  ways to do both and the research is the  way to find them what's the lowest  hanging fruit in terms of actions that  could be taken that are both politically  viable and sort of legally tenable is it  background checks is it the domestic  violence exemptions like where would you  go first well I think there are a lot of  areas to start one is waiting periods it  turns out that a lot of people who  commit suicide especially young people  do it on an impulse something bad  happens they have a big problem at  school they break up in a serious  relationship or their parents do  something to them that they don't like  and they rush out buy a gun and shoot  themselves it turns out in a study of  people who almost killed themselves but  survived that most of the young people  who shot themselves and missed their  heart or missed the critical brain parts  most of them had been thinking of  suicide not for a year not for months  not for weeks for five minutes  if you can keep them from rushing out  and buying a gun when they have that  impulse you can save their lives and you  can do this without infringing at all on  the rights of law-abiding gun owners the  NRA in another example for a long time  has said that there are laws on the  books that we need to enforce so let's  find out how we get better reporting of  people who've been convicted of domestic  violence felonies or misdemeanors right  who shouldn't be allowed to buy guns the  shooter in Sutherland Texas the Air  Force messed up they didn't report him  there many  other cases that don't get reported how  do we find out how to implement the laws  and make them effective how do we  separate guns in the hands of people who  are criminals who've been convicted of  major felonies who shouldn't have guns  how to actually get them out of their  hands and keep them out of their hands  extreme risk people who have threatened  to kill themselves how do we get guns  out of their hands and keep them out of  their hands for as long as they're at  risk  these are just three ways that we can  both protect gun rights and prevent gun  violence and there are many more ways  waiting to be discovered through  research we can't say what research  we'll find but if we set out to find  those things that will both protect gun  rights and prevent gun violence we'll  find them  we'll find them and they will get us out  of this and we'll be able to tell  lawmakers who right now are asked to  vote on things without knowing whether  they're safe or effective we'll be able  to tell them here are the data No  we might say it's not safe to arm all  teachers because maybe research shows  you'll kid kill more kids instead of  saving more lives maybe we'll give them  the data that they need to pass smart  legislation so there are many things  that will both protect gun rights and  prevent gun violence let's start finding  them we've been going far too long  without knowing what they are but  they're there for science to find we've  been speaking with mark Rosenberg  founding director of CDC's National  Center for injury prevention and control  also a retired assistant Surgeon General  mark great to talk to you again thanks  very much David it's an important  message you have to get out appreciate  it

